WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.577
There seemed to be an
implication in your-- first

00:00:17.577 --> 00:00:18.410
of all, great talks.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:21.500
Really, these are fantastic
that we're having these at Yale.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.870
So I'm saying this with love.

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.312
So the first question
is I just seem

00:00:30.312 --> 00:00:32.270
to get the implication
that you were suggesting

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:34.311
that exploratory research
shouldn't be registered

00:00:34.311 --> 00:00:36.080
and that people
should be- and what

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:37.822
I believe is that
if you're going

00:00:37.822 --> 00:00:39.530
to do exploratory
research, that's great.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:41.150
And we should be publishing
exploratory research.

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:43.108
We should be prioritizing
exploratory research.

00:00:43.108 --> 00:00:46.160
We should register that we're
doing exploratory research.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.860
And we should give a
methods history of that.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.810
I think that sometimes
people don't take a moment

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:55.340
to say what exactly,
what question

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.710
am I actually trying
to answer here.

00:00:57.710 --> 00:00:59.600
I'm just reflecting
on my own group.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.610
The time that they
spent registering

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:04.190
should be the time that they
spent organizing their ideas

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:06.297
about what exactly am I doing.

00:01:06.297 --> 00:01:07.880
And the registration
process shouldn't

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:08.963
be burdensome beyond that.

00:01:08.963 --> 00:01:11.180
But it should
enable them a pause

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.580
to say it's not just
I've got all this data,

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.500
and let me just run around.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.997
But let me take a moment to
see if I'm really a scientist.

00:01:17.997 --> 00:01:20.330
Then I should be able to at
least define what I'm doing.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:23.386
Even if that definition is
discovery and exploration.

00:01:23.386 --> 00:01:24.760
But what question
am I answering.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.620
I just wanted your
response to that.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.370
Because it just seemed
as almost you said,

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.170
well, this will slow them
down and it discourages them.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:32.427
And then, you said then if
they get negative results,

00:01:32.427 --> 00:01:33.260
it's all for naught.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:37.700
When actually, the negative
results should be informative.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.660
And so it's about, if you flip
the question, hey, I did this,

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:41.618
I didn't find anything.

00:01:41.618 --> 00:01:43.130
The next person
can build on that.

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:44.335
What are your
thoughts about that?

00:01:44.335 --> 00:01:46.043
Great, so I'll start
with the last point,

00:01:46.043 --> 00:01:49.100
which is-- so there's a model.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:52.650
Platt had a science
piece, I think 1964,

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.560
entitled strong inference.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.800
And his argument was
scientific fields advance

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:03.850
most quickly when they pose
sort of questions where

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:05.960
an experiment can answer
it, and either answer

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.610
will be informative.

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:09.289
So that is a model of science.

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:12.200
And we would love to have-- and
we should encourage researchers

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:17.210
to find those questions where
any answer, whether it's null,

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:19.220
or whatever the outcome
of the experiment,

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:21.570
it informs the community.

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:25.210
That being said, the
reality is it's often--

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:28.130
that's not the only model
of scientific inference.

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:36.940
Another model is one where--
another one, another model

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:40.090
is one where you're
sort of taking a chance.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:43.160
So you might have a hypothesis.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:44.880
So I'm being speaking
to this last point,

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.420
and then I'll come
back to this one.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.211
You have a hypothesis
that I don't believe.

00:02:48.211 --> 00:02:50.980
Maybe 1% of the community
believes your hypothesis.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:52.640
I wouldn't put money
on it, but you're

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.830
willing to put
some time into it.

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:57.109
And if you end up finding
evidence supporting of it,

00:02:57.109 --> 00:02:58.900
then that's a huge
contribution to science,

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.340
because you've
moved our beliefs.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:02.770
Our prior was
strongly against it.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:03.970
You provided evidence.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:07.870
So I think a lot of
work has that character.

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:10.780
And for that reason, if you find
evidence consistent with what

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:12.430
the community
already believed, it

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:14.680
will be harder to publish
and advance you career.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:16.300
It should absolutely
be published somewhere.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:17.424
It should enter the record.

00:03:17.424 --> 00:03:18.180
Pre-prints.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.960
Yeah, but the poor grad student
who spent three months on that

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:25.630
would have been better served
if they thought of their work

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:27.730
more in an exploratory
mode, because they were not

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:29.950
in a position to
theorize ex ante.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:32.740
So to clarify, I think
of a confirmatory

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:35.360
versus exploratory
more as a continuum.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:38.392
There's an edge where you have
pure confirmatory research,

00:03:38.392 --> 00:03:39.850
where you have a
pre-analysis plan.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:41.200
The code is all written up.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.780
And then we really know that
the p-value has the properties,

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.820
or the other tests have the
properties that statistic

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:48.550
says they should have.

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:52.150
However, we're rarely even there
with thorough preregistration.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:53.525
There's often
degrees of freedom,

00:03:53.525 --> 00:03:55.399
researcher degrees of
freedom, different ways

00:03:55.399 --> 00:03:56.652
you can analyze your data.

00:03:56.652 --> 00:03:58.360
And the more degrees
of freedom you have,

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.180
the more possibility
there are that a p-value

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:03.610
will be deflated, because
of selection for it,

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.890
sort of interesting results.

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.620
So I think my recommendation
is, yes, scholars

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:10.900
should do exploratory analysis.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:12.490
They could do
exploratory experiments.

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:16.060
Rather than feeling obliged to
make their one experiment thee

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:17.230
experiment.

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:20.110
But there's real value
to preregistering all

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:21.970
throughout the
continuum, just, I

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:24.700
think, for what you're saying,
to make it transparent,

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:30.330
what the motive inference
was that you were adopting.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.882
I agree, and I think
it's just declaring

00:04:32.882 --> 00:04:33.840
this is what I'm doing.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.330
And then I want to give
that person value for it

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:37.470
if it was worthwhile.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:40.491
Just one quick question on--
great talk about the policies,

00:04:40.491 --> 00:04:42.240
but what do we do when
the policies really

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.210
aren't being enforced?

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:47.350
So I mean, even in the
Obama administration,

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:49.227
you FOIA information
out of the FDA,

00:04:49.227 --> 00:04:51.060
you're going to be
waiting until you're old.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:53.040
You know, they can talk
about open government,

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.550
but it's not really, when
it's coming to FOiA requests.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.644
And on some hands,
we've made progress.

00:04:59.644 --> 00:05:01.060
On other hands,
we really haven't.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.050
And I think it's not consistent.

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:04.740
And with regard
to, take example,

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:05.970
what's going to go on PMI.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:08.044
The data holders, the
health care institutions,

00:05:08.044 --> 00:05:10.460
are failing to comply with
federal regulations in the High

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:12.080
Tech Act of 2009.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.200
And the government fails
to push it forward.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.950
So I think it's a
question how we are also

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:19.650
advocating for the government,
not just to make the policies,

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:21.120
but to enforce
them, so that people

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:24.940
can respect them and ensure
that they're being followed.

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:28.770
I don't know-- to the extent
this is a question about what

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:35.320
can be done, let me say two
things here, both of which

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:36.160
are unsatisfactory.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:40.030
The first is that one of
the unsatisfactory things

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:42.520
is that there were two
parallel processes that emerged

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:43.760
in the Obama administration.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.700
One was an open
government and open data

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:49.510
process run by one set of
actors, who were primarily

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:51.490
technical people.

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:53.610
And the other was a track,
that was essentially

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:56.806
the FOIA track, which is
essentially run by the lawyers.

00:05:56.806 --> 00:05:58.180
And that's one of
the challenges.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:05:59.929
So I would frequently
talk to stakeholders

00:05:59.929 --> 00:06:02.170
who would say I want
data set x, and I

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:04.600
would say don't call the
FOIA people, call the open,

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:08.230
call the CIO, call the CTO,
call the policy-- if you

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:11.920
call the other side of the
shop, you might be very lucky.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:15.990
So that's not fully an answer.

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:19.210
The truth is that you
got to work the system.

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:22.420
And what's happening-- so I
just literally submitted a grant

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:24.264
proposal to say,
to a funder, to say

00:06:24.264 --> 00:06:25.930
we've been doing all
this great training

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:28.210
with all these chief innovation
officers, chief technologists.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:30.040
We have chief data
scientists in places.

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:31.000
They get it.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:32.770
But we're not
teaching the lawyers.

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:33.820
And by the way, it's
one of the reasons

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:35.350
I'm back here at my Alma mater.

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:38.950
My home base is-- I'm at the
engineering school at NYU.

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:40.840
But you know, it's
the lawyers who

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:43.630
actually need to be
brought along for the ride

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:46.540
because they view
themselves rightly

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:49.014
as the safeguards
of due process,

00:06:49.014 --> 00:06:50.680
as the safeguarders
of the public trust.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:51.429
But you know what?

00:06:51.429 --> 00:06:54.790
The world is moving on in terms
of understanding openness.

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:57.880
By the way, we just did
a huge report on openness

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:02.710
in the grant making
process, trying

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:04.630
to push grant innovations.

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:07.930
Lots of people on board, but
the lawyers are like what?

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:09.670
So, we're catching
them by surprise

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:12.520
by not training those
people and bringing them

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:13.900
in as part of the conversation.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:18.640
So the FDA is a very,
as you know, mixed bag.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:21.096
I've been working with the FDA
for a very, very long time.

00:07:21.096 --> 00:07:22.720
We made a lot of
progress on opening up

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:24.630
some data when Josh was there.

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:27.640
He and I worked on creating a
transparency initiative FDA.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:29.140
We got some data out.

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:31.572
Sadly he left, and I left.

00:07:31.572 --> 00:07:33.280
Right now, we're
working on another piece

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:34.450
of things, which
I'll just mention,

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:35.741
to put it on your radar screen.

00:07:35.741 --> 00:07:38.260
One of the big problems we've
seen at a lot of these places

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:40.300
is data readiness.

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:42.250
So, again, it's
like people don't

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:44.500
get what to do with the
data internal to government.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:46.750
So yeah, you have a
dengue forecasting project

00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:49.270
at HHS, which is very
sophisticated in terms

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:52.060
of let's use predictive
analytics to spot dengue.

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:54.070
But in most-- I'm
working with the NHS

00:07:54.070 --> 00:07:58.732
in England, a huge organization
opening up a lot of its data.

00:07:58.732 --> 00:08:00.190
You know how many
people they have,

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:02.139
who have the title of
analyst, or anything

00:08:02.139 --> 00:08:03.430
to do with data in their title?

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:08.650
150 people in an organization
that has millions in it.

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:12.057
Now there's a lot of talent
that's hiding in plain sight,

00:08:12.057 --> 00:08:14.140
but you don't have people
in decision making roles

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:17.470
who even begin to understand
what the value is of data

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:18.640
or how to use it.

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:19.850
It's a very mixed bag.

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:22.480
The Commerce Department
here has 30 data scientists

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:24.910
sitting at the top to the
right of the secretary.

00:08:24.910 --> 00:08:27.880
Other agencies, they
don't know from data.

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:30.100
So it's partly,
you know, the FDA

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:33.010
has its own very significant
challenges in terms

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:35.260
of the outside lobbying on it.

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:40.270
But in part , there's a lot
of work to be done culturally

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:43.870
in terms of bringing
the no machines, aka,

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:46.510
the lawyers and the procurement
people along for the ride,

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:48.220
finding the people
who are data savvy,

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:50.277
and putting them in key roles.

00:08:50.277 --> 00:08:52.110
So I think that talent
is a big piece of it.

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:54.740
So I think there's
work that can be done.

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:57.040
I would not, however,
put a lot of money

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:59.770
on the next administration.

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:02.440
But we may be
pleasantly surprised

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:06.310
if we keep telling them
that open data creates jobs

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:09.470
that we can keep in the
United States, who knows.

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:12.320
Maybe we can have
some successes.

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:15.415
But I put my money on Mexico
right now, and Canada.

00:09:15.415 --> 00:09:19.030
For open date-- I
don't mean-- and moving

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:23.380
there, but on open data,
they're both better

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:25.570
than we are at the moment.

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:29.500
Well, thank you for two
very good presentations.

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:33.250
My question is to Beth.

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:36.260
In your experience
in government,

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:39.550
what is the current
status, or did you ever

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:49.010
have any conversations about
making tax return data open?

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:50.610
What's the status there?

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:56.670
And second, when
data are gathered

00:09:56.670 --> 00:09:58.920
through social
economic processes,

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:05.330
as opposed to mechanical
automatic processes,

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:08.460
you concern yourself
with a trade off,

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:14.320
between the openness of data
and the quality of data,

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:16.330
deterioration of the data.

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:19.810
Oh, tax data, I have a great
success story to report

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:21.810
that will surprise you I hope.

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:24.430
So, not the story you're
actually asking me about,

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:27.040
but nonprofit tax return data.

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:29.440
I know you want
individual taxes.

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:31.900
Let me say on the
individual tax return side,

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:33.790
I think the question
has to do with providing

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:34.810
me back my own data.

00:10:34.810 --> 00:10:38.470
So it's the story of what
Deb Estrin at Cornell Tech

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:40.090
calls small data.

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:42.100
If you're Nigel
Shadbolt at Southampton,

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:43.120
you call it my data.

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:45.280
So processes of
creating open data.

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:48.430
My data that you give back to
me, whether it's my health care

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:52.600
record or my tax return, I would
say there, the IRS is at zero.

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:57.411
Where we have made progress
is on the nonprofit tax return

00:10:57.411 --> 00:10:57.910
front.

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:01.349
So by law, nonprofits
like Yale, file their 990s

00:11:01.349 --> 00:11:03.640
with the government, at the
federal level and the state

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:04.810
level.

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:10.120
And those tax returns by law
are supposed to be made open.

00:11:10.120 --> 00:11:14.590
Now, the openness that they
were providing previously

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:17.080
was if you electronically
filed your returns,

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:22.520
the IRS would print them out,
scam them back in and then

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:24.830
put that image file on a DVD.

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:28.910
And once a year for $20,000
you could buy that DVD.

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:34.250
I'm going to fast forward a
lot of pain personally suffered

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:37.550
to say that now you can download
that data from the Amazon

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:40.310
Cloud, hooray.

00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:42.980
The other part of that story,
which is unfolding as we speak

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:45.560
is that 12 states
are working together

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:49.019
to pilot a one-stop
filing system.

00:11:49.019 --> 00:11:51.560
So instead of having to go to
every single state in which you

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:53.018
operate, you can
go to one website,

00:11:53.018 --> 00:11:55.460
click, click, click, click,
click, file your returns,

00:11:55.460 --> 00:11:58.120
and then that data will be
made automatically open.

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:00.680
We, by the way, are
making companies

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:02.810
like Guidestar a little unhappy.

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:05.780
And people whose business
model, who are like the Apple

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:08.240
iTunes of tax returns.

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:10.176
But they're adjusting,
and they'll live,

00:12:10.176 --> 00:12:12.050
and they'll provide
value added services that

00:12:12.050 --> 00:12:14.100
are not about selling the data.

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:15.980
So there is a very
happy news there.

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:19.501
For the story about how we
actually track tax data,

00:12:19.501 --> 00:12:21.500
I would point you to the
work of a scholar named

00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:26.180
Julia Lane, an economist who's
now moved to NYU from Chicago

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:28.340
this year, who's
done a lot of work

00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:32.000
on creating, getting access,
combining grants data, tax

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:34.790
data, census data, so
that we can actually

00:12:34.790 --> 00:12:37.340
track the value of government
investment in science.

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:40.160
So if the government gives
$1 to Yale for science,

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:42.400
we should be able to
know, and tax return data

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:44.990
is essential to that,
what the economic,

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:46.550
the downstream
economic impact is

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:48.560
of those investments in science.

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:50.730
So that's again a
story about data.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:52.610
You know, the "Fifty
Shades of Gray,"

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:55.970
it's not about open data,
but about responsible access

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:58.700
to data that we're
making some progress

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:03.890
on by virtue of having put some
innovation rules into the IRS.

00:13:03.890 --> 00:13:08.030
And the last part of this story
is commitments that are abroad,

00:13:08.030 --> 00:13:11.780
primarily around opening up
corporate beneficial ownership

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:15.260
data, which is partly coming
from tax returns, which

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:17.610
is knowing who's the
real owner of a company.

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:19.604
The UK has made
progress on this front

00:13:19.604 --> 00:13:21.520
and other countries are
trying to move forward

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:25.310
and actually trying to tell us
who owns the companies that we

00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:28.750
do business with, which is
a huge other consideration.

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:31.340
My question is about 1040.

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:33.580
Yeah, no, no.

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:37.600
[INAUDIBLE] we have used
for about last 20 years.

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:40.300
No, no progress, zero.

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:42.430
Nothing to say.

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:46.330
I mean, even access,
I mean, the IRS

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:48.130
has a statistical
division that does

00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:52.787
pull samples of the tax data in
order to do statistical work.

00:13:52.787 --> 00:13:54.370
I would say there's
been no investment

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:55.720
or progress on this front.

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:58.480
I mean, there's a desire just to
simplify the forms that we fill

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:02.677
out and to create simpler
tax filing systems,

00:14:02.677 --> 00:14:05.260
or even to create a default like
they have in other countries,

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:07.150
where the government
automatically

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:10.420
files my tax return
and then I correct it,

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:12.760
but we've made no
progress on this.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:16.517
Does anybody else have
better news to report?

00:14:16.517 --> 00:14:18.850
I thought I had happy-- I had
happy news on some fronts.

00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:25.290
I will be briefer in my
response, my apologies.

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:25.980
Hi, how are you?

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:27.730
Thank you very
much for the chat.

00:14:27.730 --> 00:14:30.480
I'm [INAUDIBLE], I'm a graduate
student of school of forestry.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:33.210
I wanted to ask
you about what are

00:14:33.210 --> 00:14:35.850
your thoughts about
the openness of data

00:14:35.850 --> 00:14:38.160
from government, but not
only about the raw data

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:41.880
they use for policy making,
but also about the methods they

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:46.200
use, and how can organizations
like the open data,

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:48.210
open government
partnership can actually

00:14:48.210 --> 00:14:50.610
encourage these in not only
that the United States,

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:54.000
but in other governments that
might be also producing data,

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:57.030
but at the same time, getting
to know how they actually use

00:14:57.030 --> 00:14:59.950
them to provide public policy.

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:11.470
So there are, in terms of
things like-- so there effort

00:15:11.470 --> 00:15:12.190
in sectors.

00:15:12.190 --> 00:15:14.350
So things like the open
contracting partnership

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:18.850
are working to help standardize,
to help governments know

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:21.730
how to publish their data,
their contracting data

00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:23.500
and how to
standardize that data.

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:25.720
There are working groups
that are more technical that

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:30.070
are focused on things like data
quality and data standards,

00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:32.500
including there's a
group that was stood up

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:35.540
called the Open Data
Institute out of the UK,

00:15:35.540 --> 00:15:38.110
which is co-chaired by
Nigel Shadbolt and Sir Tim

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:40.930
Berners-Lee.

00:15:40.930 --> 00:15:43.897
And they do a bit of this work,
trying to help governments

00:15:43.897 --> 00:15:44.980
with their data practices.

00:15:44.980 --> 00:15:48.010
I think partly what's
happening is a move spurred

00:15:48.010 --> 00:15:52.900
by hiring a White House level
chief data scientist trying

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:55.840
to create a community of data
scientists in government.

00:15:55.840 --> 00:15:59.050
So commerce, for example
has been in the lead

00:15:59.050 --> 00:16:01.870
and trying to
provide then guidance

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:04.360
and training for
other agencies, as you

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:08.530
see people start to replicate
creating data savvy roles

00:16:08.530 --> 00:16:09.700
within the agencies.

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:11.551
But there really is a
tremendous shortage.

00:16:11.551 --> 00:16:14.050
We see a tremendous shortage
in the private sector of people

00:16:14.050 --> 00:16:15.310
with data science training.

00:16:15.310 --> 00:16:17.850
Everybody's clamoring
for data scientists.

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:20.956
So thank goodness Yale
has this long list

00:16:20.956 --> 00:16:22.330
of things that's
getting started.

00:16:22.330 --> 00:16:24.121
But to the extent to
which we can encourage

00:16:24.121 --> 00:16:26.560
some of those people
to go do work,

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:29.044
or at least a tour of duty
with the public sector,

00:16:29.044 --> 00:16:30.460
I think it's
incredibly important.

00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:32.860
Because, you know, right now
we have to rely on the fact

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:34.720
that we're putting
out data that often

00:16:34.720 --> 00:16:38.740
is of extremely low quality,
that's poorly collected,

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:40.930
and we also don't
know-- forgetting

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:42.640
about the fact that
the methods that we

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:47.200
use to analyze that data are
still very nascent I was,

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:49.120
last week, at the
launch at Imperial

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:52.740
College of the new center for
the mathematics of precision

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:55.300
health care, which is
simply about developing

00:16:55.300 --> 00:16:57.070
the mathematical
models and methods that

00:16:57.070 --> 00:17:00.594
can be then applied to
looking at health care data.

00:17:00.594 --> 00:17:02.260
You know, it's a group
of mathematicians

00:17:02.260 --> 00:17:03.870
working with clinicians.

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:05.906
And so I think we have
a very long way to go.

00:17:05.906 --> 00:17:08.530
We particularly have a long way
to go because of the talent gap

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:09.630
that we see in government.

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:13.490
So pockets of work, and are
happy to share more by email,

00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:17.411
but one of the big-- let me just
say that the big challenge is

00:17:17.411 --> 00:17:18.869
when it continues
to be work that's

00:17:18.869 --> 00:17:20.940
done by-- if it's
done by diplomats,

00:17:20.940 --> 00:17:22.180
we don't get very far.

00:17:22.180 --> 00:17:24.599
And then one layer down
to the policy makers,

00:17:24.599 --> 00:17:28.680
but we need those policy makers
to be people who actually have

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:30.210
some data science training.

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:32.250
So next month we're
going to launch

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:35.880
a course, an online free loop
called data analytical thinking

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:38.280
aimed at mid-level government
officials who don't know

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:40.440
anything about data,
precisely to discuss,

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:42.900
to help, bring people
along for the ride.

00:17:45.485 --> 00:17:47.110
I'd like to thank
Professors Novak and.

00:17:47.110 --> 00:17:47.610
Dafoe.

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:48.801
[APPLAUSE]

